    - target: {fileID: 8473876816714486389, guid: 7c4d372918ed05a40996bf0658e7ceca, type: 3}
      propertyPath: artifactTitle
      value: "Terracotta Turtle-Shaped Vessel Lid (\u0E1D\u0E32\u0E20\u0E32\u0E0A\u0E19\u0E30\u0E14\u0E34\u0E19\u0E40\u0E1C\u0E32\u0E23\u0E39\u0E1B\u0E40\u0E15\u0E48\u0E32) "
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 8473876816714486389, guid: 7c4d372918ed05a40996bf0658e7ceca, type: 3}
      propertyPath: alternateModel
      value: 
      objectReference: {fileID: 1386213182}
    - target: {fileID: 8473876816714486389, guid: 7c4d372918ed05a40996bf0658e7ceca, type: 3}
      propertyPath: artifactDescription
      value: '- Period / Dating: 12th-16th centuries Buddhist Era (approx. 7th-11th
        centuries CE)

        - Material: Baked earthenware / Terracotta (Din Phao)

        -
        Dimensions: Diameter: 18.2 cm | Height: 4.7 cm

        - Discovery Location:
        Excavated from the archaeological site at Ban Jaleh, Yarang Ancient City,
        Pattani Province

        - Current Repository: Regional Office of Fine Arts
        No. 11, Songkhla



        Curatorial Description:

        Sculpted from
        local baked earthenware, this circular lid is modeled in the naturalistic
        form of a flattened turtle (Fa Phachana Din Phao Rup Tao). The piece features
        a broad domed carapace, four short protruding feet scored with five linear
        incisions each to denote individual toes, and a protruding turtle head extending
        forward with a subtle tongue-out expression.



        The upper surface
        of the carapace bears clear archaeological evidence of applied sculptural
        ornamentation:

        - Miniature Sculptures: Two distinct scar marks on
        the upper shell mark where miniature baby turtle figurines were originally
        affixed before detaching over time.

        - Rear Ornamentation: An additional
        scar mark at the rear edge indicates the original placement of another small
        decorative finial or miniature element of unidentified form.



        Ritual
        & Cultural Significance:

        Archaeological analysis indicates that
        this zoomorphic lid was specifically crafted to seal a consecrated earthenware
        deposit vessel containing auspicious items and sacred relics (watthu mongkhon).
        Such vessels were deposited within the structural foundations of religious
        monuments during auspicious groundbreaking and consecration rituals (kan
        wang roek).



        In ancient South and Southeast Asian cosmology, the
        turtle embodies foundational stability, cosmic support, and protection against
        negative energies. Discovered within the monument complex of Ban Jaleh, this
        artifact provides tangible material evidence of the intricate foundation-laying
        rituals and skilled ceramic craftsmanship practiced within the ancient peninsular
        trade kingdom of Langkasuka.'
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
