      value: 
      objectReference: {fileID: 60061256}
    - target: {fileID: 1140258354145779283, guid: 0e184c302cd28ff43be58db9263199ce, type: 3}
      propertyPath: artifactTitle
      value: "Stone Sculpture of a Recumbent Bull / Nandi (\u0E1B\u0E23\u0E30\u0E15\u0E34\u0E21\u0E32\u0E01\u0E23\u0E23\u0E21\u0E23\u0E39\u0E1B\u0E27\u0E31\u0E27\u0E28\u0E34\u0E25\u0E32) "
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 1140258354145779283, guid: 0e184c302cd28ff43be58db9263199ce, type: 3}
      propertyPath: alternateModel
      value: 
      objectReference: {fileID: 2028692772}
    - target: {fileID: 1140258354145779283, guid: 0e184c302cd28ff43be58db9263199ce, type: 3}
      propertyPath: artifactDescription
      value: "- Period / Dating: 11th-12th centuries Buddhist Era (approx. 6th-7th
        centuries CE)\r\n- Material: Carved sandstone (Hin Sai)\r\n- Dimensions:
        Height: 52 cm | Length: 66 cm | Width: 29.5 cm\r\n- Discovery Location: Excavated
        from Ancient Monument No. 8, Ban Jaleh, Yarang Ancient City, Pattani Province\r\n-
        Current Repository: Songkhla National Museum\r\n\r\nCuratorial Description:\r\nDiscovered
        beside an interior brick pedestal within an 8x8 meter pillared sanctuary
        hall at Ban Jaleh Monument No. 8, this monolithic sandstone sculpture portrays
        a humped zebu bull (tha non mop) resting in a tranquil, recumbent posture
        with its front legs folded beneath its chest. The sculpture features smooth,
        minimalist surfaces devoid of ornamental harnesses or jewelry, emphasizing
        the bull's naturalistic anatomy, upright ears, short horns, and a pronounced
        dorsal hump rising above the shoulders.\r\n\r\nIn ancient South and Southeast
        Asian art, the bull holds dual religious significance:\r\n- Hindu / Shaivite
        Iconography: The bull represents Nandi (Kho Nonthi), the sacred vehicle (vahana)
        and divine gatekeeper of Lord Shiva. Sculptures of Nandi are traditionally
        placed near sanctuary entrances facing inward toward the central linga.\r\n-
        Buddhist Cosmology & Lineage: The bull embodies Gotama (referencing Shakyamuni
        Buddha's lineage name) and serves as the Guardian Animal of the Western Direction.
        This directional motif draws directly from early Indian traditions\u2014such
        as Emperor Ashoka\u2019s pillar capitals featuring the elephant (East), horse
        (South), bull (West), and lion (North)\u2014as well as the carved semicircular
        moonstones (Sandakada Pahana) of Sri Lanka's Anuradhapura period (4th-16th
        centuries BE).\r\n\r\nThe discovery of sandstone chisel debitage alongside
        a bronze Avalokiteshvara head near the sanctuary base indicates that this
        monolithic sculpture was carved locally, standing as physical evidence of
        the syncretic Hindu-Buddhist devotional culture that thrived within the ancient
        maritime entrepot of Langkasuka."
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 1140258354145779283, guid: 0e184c302cd28ff43be58db9263199ce, type: 3}
      propertyPath: spatialToggleButtonAnchor
      value: 
