    - target: {fileID: 4594198701855715547, guid: b1be852542e7e1e41a6de73eaa215983, type: 3}
      propertyPath: artifactTitle
      value: "Standing Stone Buddha in Vitarka Mudra (\u0E1E\u0E23\u0E30\u0E1E\u0E38\u0E17\u0E18\u0E23\u0E39\u0E1B\u0E28\u0E34\u0E25\u0E32\u0E22\u0E37\u0E19
        \u0E1B\u0E32\u0E07\u0E41\u0E2A\u0E14\u0E07\u0E18\u0E23\u0E23\u0E21) "
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 4594198701855715547, guid: b1be852542e7e1e41a6de73eaa215983, type: 3}
      propertyPath: alternateModel
      value: 
      objectReference: {fileID: 19127875}
    - target: {fileID: 4594198701855715547, guid: b1be852542e7e1e41a6de73eaa215983, type: 3}
      propertyPath: artifactDescription
      value: "- Period / Dating: 13th-14th centuries Buddhist Era (c. 8th-9th centuries
        CE)\r\n- Material: Carved stone (Sandstone)\r\n- Dimensions: Height: 60 cm
        | Width: 16 cm\r\n- Provenance: Ban Kampong Baru (present-day Ban Jaleh),
        Yarang Ancient City, Pattani Province\r\n- Current Repository: Unknown /
        Unrecorded\r\n\r\nCuratorial Description:\r\nThis monolithic stone sculpture
        depicts a standing Buddha in a rigid upright frontal posture (samabhanga)
        resting atop an open double-lotus pedestal (bua khwam bua ngai). Both hands
        are raised symmetrically to perform the Vitarka Mudra\u2014the gesture of
        teaching, debate, and intellectual exposition. The Buddha is draped in a
        diaphanous, sheer monastic robe (civara) covering both shoulders (hom khlum)
        that clings tightly to the body contours. The frontal hem of the mantle forms
        a broad U-shaped curve extending down to the shins, revealing an inner lower
        garment that falls to the ankles.\r\n\r\nFacial and anatomical treatments
        reflect distinct regional modeling, characterized by a flat facial plane,
        downcast meditative eyes, wide lips, bold spiral hair curls, and a rounded,
        cylindrical ushnisha (cranial protuberance).\r\n\r\nWhile the image shares
        stylistic affinities with Southern peninsular sculptures from Wat Kaeo and
        Ban U-thae (Surat Thani) as well as Central Thai Dvaravati traditions, it
        demonstrates a distinctive innovation unique to the ancient Yarang workshops:
        both hands are carved resting directly against the lower abdomen at navel
        level, firmly attached to the torso rather than raised independently at chest
        or waist height. This piece stands as vital physical evidence of the refined
        local craftsmanship and Mahayana Buddhist devotional networks flourishing
        in the ancient maritime kingdom of Langkasuka."
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 4594198701855715547, guid: b1be852542e7e1e41a6de73eaa215983, type: 3}
      propertyPath: spatialToggleButtonAnchor
      value: 
      objectReference: {fileID: 1201916791}
