      objectReference: {fileID: 720074331}
    - target: {fileID: 4594198701855715547, guid: b1be852542e7e1e41a6de73eaa215983, type: 3}
      propertyPath: artifactTitle
      value: "Bronze Statue of the Sun God Surya (\u0E1E\u0E23\u0E30\u0E2A\u0E38\u0E23\u0E34\u0E22\u0E30\u0E2A\u0E33\u0E23\u0E34\u0E14)"
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 4594198701855715547, guid: b1be852542e7e1e41a6de73eaa215983, type: 3}
      propertyPath: alternateModel
      value: 
      objectReference: {fileID: 107884317}
    - target: {fileID: 4594198701855715547, guid: b1be852542e7e1e41a6de73eaa215983, type: 3}
      propertyPath: artifactDescription
      value: "- Period / Dating: 15th-16th centuries Buddhist Era (approx. 10th-11th
        centuries CE)\r\n- Material: Cast bronze\r\n- Dimensions: Height: 20.30 cm
        | Width: 10.30 cm\r\n- Discovery Location: Ban Kuwing, Sakho Bon Sub-district,
        Mayo District, Pattani Province (approx. 7 km east of Yarang Ancient City)\r\n-
        Current Repository: Unknown / Unrecorded\r\n\r\nCuratorial Description:\r\nCast
        in bronze, this intricate sculptural triad depicts Surya, the Hindu Sun God
        and supreme deity of the Saura sect. The composition features Surya standing
        in a rigid upright frontal pose (samabhanga) atop a lotus pedestal mounted
        over his celestial single-wheeled chariot. The chariot is drawn by seven
        galloping horses\u2014with the central steed emerging directly from the wheel's
        hub\u2014and is steered by the divine dawn charioteer, Aruna.\r\n\r\nSurya
        is flanked by his celestial retinue and personifications of dawn:\r\n- Pingala
        & Goddess Usha (Right): The attendant Pingala stands in a graceful dvibhanga
        posture holding a writing implement and an inkpot, fronted by a relief image
        of the dawn goddess Usha, the dispeller of darkness.\r\n- Danda & Goddess
        Pratyusha (Left): The attendant Danda stands in a dvibhanga pose holding
        a ritual attribute (possibly a conch), fronted by the partially preserved
        form of Goddess Pratyusha.\r\n- Regalia & Attributes: Surya wears a tall
        conical jewel crown (Kiritamukuta), a bare upper torso, fitted trousers (sanapphlao),
        and knee-high riding boots. Both hands are raised at hip level to hold stemmed
        lotus bouquets that rise symmetrically to the level of his head.\r\n- Aureole
        & Royal Canopy: The grouping is framed by an openwork arched halo (prabhavali)
        bordered with bead moulding and stylized leaf-like flame rays, surmounted
        at the peak by a royal tiered parasol (chattr) draped with fluttering banners
        and garlands.\r\n\r\nArtistic & Cultural Significance:\r\nStylistically,
        this sculpture exhibits strong affinities with the 10th-11th century CE Pala-period
        art of Northeast India (Bengal). Discovered in the immediate vicinity of
        Yarang Ancient City, the artifact provides key archaeological evidence of
        the active practice of the Saura tradition and highlights the extensive maritime
        trade and religious networks connecting the Indian subcontinent with the
        ancient peninsular kingdom of Langkasuka."
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 4594198701855715547, guid: b1be852542e7e1e41a6de73eaa215983, type: 3}
      propertyPath: spatialToggleButtonAnchor
      value: 
      objectReference: {fileID: 1725989259}
