      objectReference: {fileID: 1210050529}
    - target: {fileID: 8473876816714486389, guid: 7c4d372918ed05a40996bf0658e7ceca, type: 3}
      propertyPath: artifactTitle
      value: "Tiered Umbrella Spire of a Miniature Terracotta Stupa (\u0E0A\u0E34\u0E49\u0E19\u0E2A\u0E48\u0E27\u0E19\u0E2A\u0E16\u0E39\u0E1B\u0E14\u0E34\u0E19\u0E40\u0E1C\u0E32\u0E2A\u0E48\u0E27\u0E19\u0E09\u0E31\u0E15\u0E23\u0E27\u0E25\u0E35)"
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 8473876816714486389, guid: 7c4d372918ed05a40996bf0658e7ceca, type: 3}
      propertyPath: alternateModel
      value: 
      objectReference: {fileID: 113814696}
    - target: {fileID: 8473876816714486389, guid: 7c4d372918ed05a40996bf0658e7ceca, type: 3}
      propertyPath: artifactDescription
      value: "Period / Dating: 12th\u201314th centuries Buddhist Era (approx. 7th\u20139th
        centuries CE)  \r\n\r\n    Material: Fine-grained baked earthenware / Terracotta
        (Din Phao Nuea La-iat)  \r\n\r\n    Dimensions: Total Height: 46 cm | Base
        Diameter (Bottom Plate): 16.7 cm  \r\n\r\n    Provenance / Discovery Site:
        Ban Jaleh Monument Complex, Yarang Ancient City, Pattani Province (Surveyed
        by Anan Wattananikorn, B.E. 2505\u20132516 / 1962\u20131973)  \r\n\r\n   
        Current Repository: Princess Galyani Vadhana Institute of Cultural Studies,
        Prince of Songkla University, Pattani Campus (Catalog Item 23)  \r\n\r\nCuratorial
        Description\r\nThis modular baked clay artifact represents an authentic chatra-valee
        (tiered umbrella spire) crafted to crown the superstructure of a miniature
        terracotta votive stupa (Sathup Din Phao).  \r\n\r\nThe spire is constructed
        from 7 concentric, ring-shaped umbrella plates (chatra) engineered with raised
        central cylindrical sleeves. These interlocking components are stacked vertically
        along a central axis in decreasing diameter from the wide base plate to the
        narrow summit, forming an elegant graduated conical silhouette. The pinnacle
        terminates in a tall, elongated pointed lotus-bud finial (bua tum song sung). 
        \r\n\r\nArchaeological & Spiritual Significance\r\nDiscovered within the
        ancient architectural complex of Ban Jaleh, this artifact showcases the specialized
        modular ceramic production techniques developed in the Yarang workshops during
        the height of the ancient maritime kingdom of Langkasuka.  \r\n\r\nIn Buddhist
        cosmology, the multi-tiered chatra-valee symbolizes the ascending celestial
        realms (heavens) and royal-spiritual sovereignty. Miniature stupas crowned
        with these multi-tiered spires were produced in vast numbers as devotional
        merit-making offerings and consecrated foundation deposits, reflecting the
        widespread presence of Mahayana Buddhism and the Cetiyavada school in early
        peninsular Southeast Asia."
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
